{"name":"Overnight Oats","hex":"#fbf0db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"overnight oats","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Oatmeal prepared from raw oats soaked overnight in milk or a substitute."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/overnight_oats"}],"entries":[{"title":"Muesli","blocks":[{"text":"Muesli is a cold Swiss dish that has become a common breakfast cereal prepared without cooking. Developed around 1900 by Swiss physician Maximilian Bircher-Benner for patients in his hospital in Switzerland, it is also consumed for supper as Birchermüesli complet, 'Bircher's complete little mush', i.e., muesli with café complet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Dorset_Cereals_muesli.jpg/330px-Dorset_Cereals_muesli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muesli","wikidata":"Q52406"},{"title":"Oat","blocks":[{"text":"The oat, sometimes called the common oat, is a species of cereal grass (Avena) grown for fodder and for its seed, which is known by the same name. Oats appear to have been domesticated as a secondary crop, as their seeds resembled those of other cereals closely enough for them to be included by early cultivators. Oats tolerate cold winters less well than cereals such as wheat, barley, and rye, but need less summer heat and more rain, making them important in areas such as Northwest Europe that have cool, wet summers. They can tolerate low-nutrient and acid soils. Oats grow thickly and vigorously, allowing them to outcompete many weeds, and compared to other cereals are relatively free from diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/AvenaSativa3.jpg/330px-AvenaSativa3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oat","wikidata":"Q12104"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/